{
  "image_name": "7cbfe6cbb010b339d5c4e2618b2beb24.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two perovskite solar-cell stack designs aimed at improving reverse-bias tolerance. In both, the device is built on glass with a bottom ITO electrode, a PTAA hole-transport layer, and a central perovskite absorber, but the upper contact architecture differs.\n\nOn the left, Huang and colleagues use a multi-layered cathode above the perovskite, consisting of thin LiF, C60, SnO2, and ITO layers capped with Cu. This layered top contact is highlighted as allowing breakdown only beyond about 20 V reverse bias, suggesting stronger protection against reverse-bias damage.\n\nOn the right, Ginger and colleagues instead use a thick polymeric anode: a noticeably thicker PTAA layer beneath the perovskite, and above it a C60/BCP/Au top contact. This configuration also improves robustness, with reverse-bias breakdown occurring beyond roughly 15 V.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes that interface and electrode engineering—either by inserting multiple functional cathode layers or by thickening a polymeric transport/contact layer—can substantially raise the reverse-bias breakdown voltage of perovskite devices. The wavy boundaries visually suggest layered thin-film deposition rather than perfectly flat interfaces.",
